# Keys an ephemeral session may not override.
#
# THERE ARE NONE, AND THAT IS THE DECISION RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.
#
# Markus, 2026-09-24: "i strongly feel we shouldn't lock any of the gnome
# settings. that's enforcing policy, something we're generally reluctant to do.
# and these are ephemeral sessions. the user isn't going to hurt anyone but
# themselves, if they misconfigure gnome."
#
# This file previously locked nine keys in two groups, and both reasons were
# good ones. They are recorded here because the next person to reach for a lock
# will have a reason at least as good, and should have to argue past these
# rather than rediscover them.
#
#   THE BACKGROUND (six keys). "The background is the cue that this session
#   keeps nothing." True, and it made the wallpaper load-bearing for a message
#   that is not about the wallpaper. That cue belongs on the client page
#   surround, which the visitor does not own and cannot remove -- see
#   private/DESIGN-per-session-background.md. A cue that has to be defended by
#   taking a setting away from its owner is in the wrong place.
#
#   THE SCREENSAVER (three keys). "Locking the screen strands the user: there
#   is no password to unlock it with." That one is NOT policy -- it is an
#   unrecoverable state, and it is the case that argues hardest against an empty
#   file. It is still the user harming only themselves, and in an ephemeral
#   session the loss is bounded by what the session was going to discard anyway.
#   His ruling covers it; the honest repair if it bites is to make the state
#   recoverable, not to forbid reaching it.
#
# So: locking is for a key where a session changing it strands its user with no
# way back AND no better repair exists. Neither of the groups above met the
# second half. If you are about to add one, say which of those two tests it
# passes, in this file, beside the key.
